        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Symmetric about the $y$-axis, with vertical asymptotes $x=\\pm1$, horizontal asymptote $y=1$, and a pointed local maximum at $(0,-1)$. There are no $x$-intercepts.",
          "worked": "From part a,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=1+\\frac{2}{x-1},\\qquad x\\ne1.\\end{align*}$$\nSketch this translated reciprocal graph. Keep its portion for $x\\ge0$ and reflect that portion across the $y$-axis.\n\nThe reflected graph has vertical asymptotes $x=-1$ and $x=1$, and approaches $y=1$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$. At $x=0$, $y=-1$, giving a pointed local maximum at $(0,-1)$. Since $|x|+1>0$, there are no $x$-intercepts. The domain excludes $x=\\pm1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
